---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getPost } from "../../data/journal";

const post = getPost("every-control")!;
---

<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | EyesRhythm`}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  published={post.date}
  image={post.cover}
  imageAlt={post.coverAlt}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="cover">
      <img
        src={post.cover}
        alt={post.coverAlt}
        width="1000"
        height="1724"
      />
      <div class="cover__shade"></div>
      <div class="cover__copy">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>{post.kicker}</p>
        <h1 class="display">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{post.description}</p>
        <p class="meta">{post.read} · 4 November 2026</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          EyesRhythm is a small window that sits beside your work. It watches
          blinks through the camera already on the computer, keeps the video
          on this machine, and gives you a few controls when you want them.
          This is a walk through those controls, in the order you meet them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pictures below are the real app. The numbers on your screen will
          be your own. A score of 84 here is just the afternoon this guide was
          taken. Sit the way you usually work, and follow the
          <a href="/journal/camera-setup">camera setup</a>
          once so the lens can see your eyes.
        </p>

        <h2>The first question</h2>
        <p>
          Each time you open EyesRhythm, a short screen comes up before the
          window. A thin blue bar at the top shrinks for three minutes. If
          you walk away, the app continues on its own. The corner shows how
          much time is left.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the computer has more than one camera and you have never chosen
          one, this screen asks which camera to use first. Pick the webcam you
          sit in front of, or <strong>Hybrid</strong> if you want every desk
          camera at once. Hybrid uses more of the processor. A phone camera is
          left out. Then press <strong>Continue</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next it asks how your eyes feel right now. This stays on the
          computer. It is a self-check, and it chooses the sentence you will
          see later under Today’s plan.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/splash-comfort.jpg"
            alt="The opening screen with five comfort choices. Comfortable is selected, and Continue is at the bottom."
            width="1040"
            height="896"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Pick one line. Continue stays quiet until you do.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Comfortable</strong> — no dryness, strain, or irritation.
            The plan later asks you to hold about 15–20 blinks a minute and
            leave Alert in the background.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Mild symptoms</strong> — a little dryness or tired eyes.
            The plan suggests a full blink, and a short Practice if you want
            one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Moderate discomfort</strong> — gritty, burning, or tired
            eyes. The plan suggests looking away on purpose, and a Practice
            before a long stretch of screens.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Marked irritation</strong> — dryness, pain, or light
            sensitivity that stays. The plan is a gentle day, and a reminder
            to see an eye-care professional if pain persists.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Prefer not to say</strong> — skip the check. The plan
            falls back to the everyday aim of 15–20 blinks a minute.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Press <strong>Continue</strong>, or press Enter. The choice is
          remembered for the next launch.
        </p>

        <h2>Three notes, then Start</h2>
        <p>
          The last opening screen is three short notes. Read them once. You
          can press <strong>Start</strong> straight away, or wait and the app
          will start when the timer ends.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/splash-notices.jpg"
            alt="Three notes before start: on-device privacy, a wellness disclaimer, and a short explanation of the Ocular Rhythm Index. A Start button sits at the bottom."
            width="1040"
            height="966"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Start begins the session. The timer will do it for you if you wait.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          The notes say the same thing the app keeps saying in quieter places.
          The camera and the scores stay on this computer. Nothing is uploaded,
          and there is no account. EyesRhythm is a wellness companion, and it
          is there to nudge a blink rhythm during screen work. The third note
          introduces the Ocular Rhythm Index, the 0–100 score you will see on
          the blue card in a moment.
        </p>

        <h2>The window</h2>
        <p>
          After Start, the window is the whole product. Along the top is the
          name, and a light that tells you the camera state. <strong>Live</strong>
          means a face is in view. <strong>Live · looking for you</strong>
          means the camera is on and still waiting for a face.
          <strong>Connecting…</strong> means the window is still reaching the
          engine on this computer.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/dashboard.jpg"
            alt="The full EyesRhythm window: Atlas selected, Today’s plan, four score cards, a quote, and the Beep, Visual, and Practice buttons."
            width="1000"
            height="1724"
          />
          <figcaption>
            This is the everyday window. Atlas in the middle, scores below,
            three buttons along the bottom.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          The face in the middle is a stand-in, so you do not have to watch
          yourself. The pill on the forehead reads <strong>Eyes open</strong>,
          <strong>Eyes closed</strong>, <strong>Off-screen</strong>, or
          <strong>No face</strong>. The number at the top right is
          <strong>blinks today</strong>. It counts while a face is in view,
          and it starts again after midnight.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the face are six views. One is selected at a time, and the
          choice is remembered next time you open the app.
        </p>

        <h2>Atlas, Lumen, and Vesper</h2>
        <p>
          These three are the same companion with a different face. The eyes
          close when a blink is counted. Pick the one you like looking at.
          Atlas is the warm default. Lumen is pale. Vesper is the cool blue one.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/lumen.jpg"
            alt="Lumen selected, a pale face with the No face pill and the blinks-today count."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>Lumen. Same job as Atlas, a lighter face.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/vesper.jpg"
            alt="Vesper selected, a blue face with the six view buttons underneath."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>Vesper. The blue face, still the same scores underneath.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Lens</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Lens</strong> replaces the face with the camera picture, so
          you can check the view. You want both eyes in the frame, light on
          your face, and the pill changing from No face to Eyes open. The
          small camera mark on the picture is only a label. When the check
          looks right, switch back to Atlas, Lumen, Vesper, Trace, or Poise.
          You do not need to watch yourself for the rest of the day.
        </p>
        <p>
          The letter <strong>C</strong> jumps to Lens, and C again jumps back
          to Atlas.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/lens.jpg"
            alt="Lens selected, showing the camera view with a No face pill because the chair is empty. The six view buttons sit underneath."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Lens, with an empty chair. The pill says No face until someone
            sits down in front of the camera.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Trace</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Trace</strong> is the last five minutes of your Ocular Rhythm
          Index, drawn as a line. Now is on the left. Five minutes ago is on
          the right. The red dotted line is the alert line, the same number as
          the ORI threshold in Settings, 50 unless you move it. An orange dot
          marks a counted blink. The line pauses when your face leaves the
          picture or you look away far enough to count as off-screen.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/trace.jpg"
            alt="The Trace view, an empty five-minute chart with a red dotted line at 50 and Trace selected."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Trace while nobody is in front of the camera, so the line is
            waiting. The red dots sit at the alert line.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Poise</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Poise</strong> is about where you point your head during the
          day, in slices of 30 degrees. Green is a direction you have already
          used. Red is still waiting. The pulsing slice is the one to try
          next. A short turn up, down, left, or right, then back to the
          screen, is the habit it is there to encourage. A long turn still
          counts as off-screen for the blink score, because that score is
          about time at the work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bar under the dial runs from <strong>rarely</strong> to
          <strong>often</strong>. The line beside it names where you have
          looked most, and where a little more turning would help. The
          percentage in the middle is how balanced the day has been so far.
          <strong>Reset</strong> clears today’s map and starts the dial again.
          Leave it alone unless you want a fresh picture.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/poise.jpg"
            alt="The Poise dial, with a green slice to the left, a 12 percent balance, and a Reset button."
            width="1904"
            height="1544"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Poise on a day that has mostly looked left. The note says to turn
            a little more upward.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Less, and Settings</h2>
        <p>
          Two buttons sit under the views. <strong>Less</strong> hides the
          scores, the plan, and the title, and the window shrinks to the face
          plus the three buttons at the bottom. It becomes <strong>More</strong>
          when the window is small. The letter <strong>D</strong> does the
          same thing. Use Less when you want the companion out of the way.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/compact.jpg"
            alt="The compact window: a note that the camera cannot see a face yet, Atlas, More, Settings, and the three bottom buttons."
            width="1000"
            height="1022"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Less hides the cards. More brings them back. The pale note appears
            when the camera is on and still cannot see a face.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          <strong>Settings</strong> opens a panel beside the window, with
          three tabs: Settings, Reports, and About. The button then reads
          <strong>Hide settings</strong>. The letter <strong>W</strong> opens
          and closes that panel. The panel scrolls if your window is short.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the camera stays on and no face appears for a few seconds, a pale
          note shows up above the face. It asks you to tap Lens, or to change
          the camera in Settings. That note is a hint, and it goes away once
          a face is found.
        </p>

        <h2>Today’s plan and the four cards</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Today’s plan</strong> is the sentence chosen by the comfort
          question. It does not change any slider. It only reminds you what
          kind of day you said this was.
        </p>
        <p>
          Four cards sit under the plan. They update while you are in view.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Ocular Rhythm Index</strong> is the main score, from 0 to
            100. It mixes how often you blink with how evenly those blinks are
            spaced, about 70 percent rate and 30 percent rhythm once the
            rhythm has had a few blinks to warm up. Higher is steadier. The
            card stays quiet, and shows a dash, until a face is in view and a
            few blinks have landed. There is a longer explanation in
            <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">why the blink count is not enough</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Screen time</strong> is how long a face has been visible
            today. Looking away, or dropping out of the picture, pauses it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>FPS</strong> is how fast the camera pipeline is running.
            It is a health light for the app, not a score for your eyes. A
            small number usually means the camera is struggling, or another
            app is holding it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Look angle</strong> is how far your face is turned from
            straight at the camera, in degrees. Straight is the comfortable
            end. The subtitle says the direction, or <strong>off-screen</strong>
            when the turn is large enough that blinks pause.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The quote card</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Information &amp; Quote</strong> is the wide grey card. On
          an ordinary day it lists the three bottom buttons in one line each,
          then a short quotation that changes about every half minute. During
          Practice the tips step aside for the practice instruction, a timer,
          and a thin progress bar.
        </p>

        <h2>Beep, Visual, and Practice</h2>
        <p>
          Three buttons run along the bottom. Each one has a small letter, and
          that letter does the same thing from the keyboard while you are not
          typing in a field.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Beep (B)</strong> makes a short sound on every counted
            blink. The dot on the button lights when it is on, and the button
            flashes when a blink lands. Leave it off if you want silence.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Visual (A)</strong> is the alert, and the alert is always
            on. The button’s name is the mode. Visual is the quiet default: the
            window takes on a soft orange glow when your score has been under
            the line for long enough. Click it, or press A, and the name
            changes to <strong>Sound</strong>. Sound plays a short tone, at
            most once a minute, in that same situation. Click again to come
            back to Visual.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Practice (P)</strong> opens a two-minute blink coach. More
            on that screen below.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Under the buttons, one line points out that EyesRhythm is for general
          wellness, and that the full wording lives in Settings, then About.
        </p>

        <h2>The Settings tab</h2>
        <p>
          Open Settings. The right-hand panel is where the numbers live.
          Everything you move here takes effect straight away. The help line
          under each slider tells you what that slider is for. The number on
          the right is the value in use on this computer.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/settings.jpg"
            alt="Settings open beside the window: camera menu, ORI threshold, poor-rhythm minutes, writing angle, turn angle, blink sensitivity, and a list of recent changes."
            width="2000"
            height="2272"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Settings beside the companion. In this picture the turn angle and
            the blink sensitivity have already been moved from the starting
            values.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          <strong>Restore defaults</strong> puts every slider back to the
          values the app ships with. The camera choice stays as you set it.
          The button reads Restoring… for a moment while it saves.
        </p>
        <h3>Camera</h3>
        <p>
          The menu lists the webcams EyesRhythm can see. In the picture it is
          the built-in FaceTime camera. Choose another plugged-in camera if
          you use one. If more than one desk camera is connected, the menu
          also offers <strong>Hybrid</strong>, which runs them together and
          counts a blink from the front-facing view. Hybrid asks more of the
          processor. Changing this restarts the engine, so the Live light may
          blink off and on.
        </p>
        <h3>ORI threshold</h3>
        <p>
          This slider runs from 20 to 80. It starts at <strong>50</strong>.
          The score at the line, and anything under it, counts as a poor
          stretch for the alert. The red dotted line on Trace moves with it.
          A higher line means the alert speaks up sooner. A lower line waits
          until the rhythm is further down.
        </p>
        <h3>Poor-rhythm minutes</h3>
        <p>
          This slider runs from 1 to 5 minutes. It starts at
          <strong>3 min</strong>. It is how many minutes of low score, inside
          the last five, have to add up before Visual glows or Sound beeps.
          It is the wait before the nudge, and it is separate from how long
          the sound itself lasts. 1 nudges sooner. 5 waits until almost the
          whole Trace window has been low.
        </p>
        <h3>Writing angle</h3>
        <p>
          This slider runs from 10° to 40°. It starts at <strong>20°</strong>.
          It is how far downward still counts as looking at the screen. At 20°,
          a glance of 20° or more is treated as off-screen: blinks, the
          rhythm score, and the alert pause, and Trace cuts. A lower number
          is stricter, which suits work close to the keyboard. A higher number,
          toward 30–40°, keeps counting while you look down at notes.
        </p>
        <h3>Turn angle</h3>
        <p>
          This slider runs from 20° to 80°. It starts at <strong>50°</strong>.
          It is how far left or right still counts as looking at the screen.
          Past that angle, counting and the alert pause, and Trace cuts. Lower
          is stricter. Higher allows a wider glance to a side screen. In the
          picture the slider sits at 36°, which is a choice someone already
          made on that computer, and it is tighter than the start.
        </p>
        <h3>Blink sensitivity</h3>
        <p>
          This slider runs from 0.10 to 0.30. It starts at <strong>0.18</strong>.
          It is how far the eyelids have to close, past your open-eye baseline,
          before a blink counts. A lower number catches a smaller or incomplete
          blink. A higher number asks for a clearer closure, so a soft blink
          or a blink behind glasses may be missed, and stray twitches are less
          likely to count. The picture shows 0.12, again a choice already made
          on that computer. If your count feels too eager, or too shy, this is
          the slider to nudge, a little at a time.
        </p>
        <h3>Recent changes</h3>
        <p>
          Under the sliders is a short log of what was changed, with the date
          and the old value and the new one. It is written to a file called
          audit.json on this computer. Nothing in that list is uploaded. A
          fresh install says that no changes have been made yet.
        </p>

        <h2>Reports</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Reports</strong> tab is the history. Three buttons at
          the top choose the shape of the chart: <strong>Daily</strong>,
          <strong>Weekly</strong>, and <strong>Monthly</strong>. Both charts
          on the page follow that choice.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports.jpg"
            alt="The Reports tab on Daily, with an ocular rhythm chart across 24 hours and a screen-time chart below it."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Daily. Each bar is a slice of the day. The green dotted line is
            the trained-eye target at 75. The red dotted line is the alert
            line at 50.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Under the three buttons is the period. The arrows step a day, a
          week, or a month. The label in the middle opens a small calendar.
          <strong>Today</strong> jumps back to the current period. You cannot
          step into the future. Days that already have a record carry a small
          mark, so you can see where the history lives.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports-calendar.jpg"
            alt="The Reports calendar open on November 2026, with the 4th selected and dots on days that have records."
            width="2000"
            height="2388"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The date label opens this calendar. Pick a day, or a week by
            choosing any day inside it.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          <strong>Ocular rhythm</strong> on Daily is the average score in each
          five-minute slice of the 24 hours. Weekly is one bar for each day,
          Monday through Sunday. Monthly is one bar for each day of the month.
          Under the chart, the page totals the mean score and the blinks in
          that period.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Screen time</strong> on Daily is the minutes you were looking
          at the screen in each five-minute slice. Weekly and Monthly switch
          to hours. The totals underneath name the time, the peak slice or the
          daily average, and, on the longer views, how many days have a record.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports-weekly.jpg"
            alt="Weekly reports for 2 to 8 November 2026, with Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday filled in."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Weekly. Empty days stay blank. Today jumps back to the week you
            are in.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports-monthly.jpg"
            alt="Monthly reports for November 2026, with bars on the first few days of the month."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Monthly. The arrows move a month at a time, and the label opens a
            grid of month names.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>About</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>About</strong> tab is the identity of this copy. It names
          the product, the version, and what the app is for. The disclaimer is
          the full wellness wording: EyesRhythm is not a medical device, the
          scores are estimates, and lasting discomfort belongs with a licensed
          eye-care professional. Support is
          <a href="mailto:support@eyesrhythm.com">support@eyesrhythm.com</a>.
          Notices name the open-source licenses that travel with the app.
          Open the disclosure at the bottom if you want the license text itself.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/about.jpg"
            alt="The About tab, with product, version 1.5.0, description, disclaimer, support email, and notices."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>
            About. The version here is 1.5.0. Yours is whatever copy you installed.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Practice</h2>
        <p>
          Press <strong>Practice</strong>, or the letter P. The window fills
          with a dark screen. For about 20 seconds you can read the instruction:
          follow the moving ball with your eyes, blink gently on each beep, and
          keep your head still. A large number counts those seconds down. A
          line under it shows how much of the wait is left.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/practice.jpg"
            alt="The Practice screen counting down from 19 seconds, with End practice and the Esc hint at the top."
            width="1800"
            height="1280"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The quiet 20 seconds before the ball starts. End practice, or Esc,
            leaves at any time.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Then the coach runs for two minutes. A ball moves, a line ties it to
          the middle of the screen, and a beep asks for a blink about every
          three seconds. The corner shows the time remaining. When the two
          minutes end, a short results card shows the blink rate and the
          regularity from that session, then the screen closes on its own.
          <strong>End practice</strong>, or Esc, closes it sooner. The bottom
          of the main window, if you can see it behind the coach, swaps its
          tips for the same instruction and the same timer.
        </p>

        <h2>The letters</h2>
        <p>
          These work when the window is focused, and they step aside while you
          are typing in a field.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Key</th>
              <th>What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>B</td>
              <td>Beep on each counted blink, on or off</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A</td>
              <td>Switch the alert between Visual and Sound</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>P</td>
              <td>Start Practice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Esc</td>
              <td>End Practice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>D</td>
              <td>Less or More</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>W</td>
              <td>Open or hide Settings</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>C</td>
              <td>Lens, or back to Atlas</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Enter</td>
              <td>Continue or Start, on the opening screens</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Then leave it</h2>
        <p>
          Once the camera can see you, and the alert is in the mode you want,
          the window can sit in Less and stay quiet. The scores keep going.
          Open Settings when a slider needs a nudge, Reports when you want the
          shape of a day, and Practice when you want two minutes of deliberate
          blinks. That is the whole set.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="back">
        <a href="/journal">All journal</a>
        ·
        <a href="/blink-rate-detector">Blink rate detector</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/camera-setup">Set up your camera so blinks count</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">Why the blink count is not enough</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cover {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: min(72vh, 640px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    color: #fff;
    background: #1d1d1f;
  }

  .cover img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
